wincan 1.1.0 (2026-11-25)
  A smooth first start: with no settings wincan asks where the candles come from (a small box); with an https:// server and no key it offers to make
  the computer's key (one Yes or No; "no" is remembered), keeps it in Windows' key storage, and puts the command for the server's administrator on
  the clipboard (candled --authorize SHA256:...).  wincan --gen-key makes the key without any question.  The PowerShell installer is optional.
  Plain HTTP is for this computer only in the client too: wincan refuses a plain connection to any other host (candled already did).  There is no
  fallback from TLS to plain HTTP, in either program, by decision: client and server are the same version and both encrypted.

wincan 1.0.0 (2026-11-24)
  The first release of the package: candled, the candle server for Linux (Debian, Ubuntu, Fedora and relatives, Alpine, WSL2), and wincan.exe,
  the candle window for the Windows desktop, with an installer for each.
  Security: connections are encrypted (TLS 1.2 or later) and every client proves who it is with a key of its own, as an IRC bouncer's certfp
  does: the client makes its key and certificate, the server is told the SHA-256 fingerprint (candled --authorize), the client pins the server's
  self-signed certificate.  candled listens on the loopback address by default and refuses plain HTTP on any other unless told.
  candled has a configuration file, candled.conf(5), in obconf notation, read by a parser generated by hbnf (grammars/candled.hbnf; the build does not
  need hbnf); command-line options override it.  -D/--daemon and -P/--pidfile; --help and --version as the GNU standards ask.
  wincan: HTTPS with the key in the user's certificate store; f for full screen; the pointer snaps to candles and locks onto lines; the candle's
  date and time under the mouse; the heat bars show their per cent and price range; volume candles do not overlap; the keyboard follows the mouse.
  Packaging: GNU autotools (make distcheck), man pages for every program and file format, the Windows installer (PowerShell), the Linux installer,
  Debian packages (RPM and Alpine packaging are written, not tested).

Development before the first release (2026-11-20 to 2026-11-24):
  First version: candled (JSON candles from the collector's framed .bin files, rolling aggregation, nine timeframes) and
  wincan.exe (frameless 300x175 always-on-top window, entry price and size with profit and loss in percent and dollars,
  tray icon, hotkey, keyboard taken on click and given back when the mouse leaves).
  Connections are kept open (HTTP keep-alive, a thread for each); the window keeps one connection, finds the server's address once, and
  keeps what it fetched for each coin and timeframe, so changing back is instant.  /pairs lists only files written in the last three days.
  candled --backfill DIR: a minute comes from --dir where it has data, else from the backfill (spot candles for the time before the perpetual
  collector started); indicators in the window (Bollinger bands 20/2, 9 and 200 moving averages), each switched by a small dot at the bottom; the
  window starts at the top left of the screen and is kept fully on a screen.
  Polling: once the history is whole, the window asks once a second for only the last two bars with the signature it holds; the server answers
  "unchanged" in a few bytes when nothing moved, and a settled bar is replaced by its final values as the next one starts.  The newest candle is drawn like the others, with black space to its right.
  The project and the Windows program are now called wincan (wincan.exe); the server is still candled.  e opens a position editor (price and amount, Tab between them; Enter or the mouse
  leaving the window ends it); x closes the trade at the mark and logs it with the best and worst excursions; q quits; one copy only; the chart stays when the connection drops.
  candled keeps every loaded pair's windows up to date in the background (a minute for all, 100 ms for the one being watched) and has /range for the highest and lowest since a time.
  Candles: forward-filled 100 ms candles no longer make a minute's open, high or low; garbage frames are ignored.
  The position editor has leverage and realized P&L as well (it opens at the box last edited and can be reopened until x closes the trade); the percentage shown is the return on the margin, the dollars include what was realized; the trade log has the new columns.
  candled --db FILE reads a database of 1-minute candles (authoritative except for the last two minutes, where the ticks are used); wincan shows the unrealized profit and its return on the initial margin as its large figures and the realized and net apart, as KuCoin does; the server address is kept so that a slow lookup of a .local name no longer shows as no connection.
  candled --live-bins DIR reads the websocket daemon's one-minute files: for closed minutes the order is the database, the exchange's own candles from those files, then the ticks (which come first only for the last two minutes).
  A fourth toggle dot switches between standard candles and volume candles (width in proportion to volume).  The ticks are used only for the candle still forming; the collector's markers carry milliseconds, so the open of the forming candle is closer to the exchange's.
  Volume candles follow the width rule measured from KuCoin's own chart (never thinner than standard; max(1, -0.92 + 1.907 sqrt(volume/median)) times the standard width; overlapping), not a guessed cap.
  Volume candles: the width rule refitted on a second KuCoin screenshot (88 candles): logarithmic against the mean volume.
  candled --official-klines: KuCoin's own final candle replaces a closed minute within seconds, and the mark price (every second) is served as "mark"; wincan's P&L and a trade closed with x use the mark, as KuCoin does, so the P&L moves every second.
  Dragging: the window may be taken partly off the screen, and snaps flush to an edge of the usable screen within 14 pixels of it.
  Dragging: wincan does its own drag (no drift); any edge of the window snaps to any edge of the usable screen within 25 pixels, from inside or outside.
  The snap distance is 20 pixels (25 was tried).
  The snap distance is 10 pixels, which scales with the screen's scaling (15 physical pixels at 150%).
  The snap distance is 12 pixels at 100% scaling (18 physical pixels at 150%).
  The snap distance is 11 pixels at 100% scaling (about 17 physical pixels at 150%).
  wincan reconnects by itself after the computer suspends and resumes.
  The heat strip along the bottom of wincan: modelled long and short liquidations at 1, 2, 5, 10 and 20 per cent, with the centre of gravity (two ways).
  Arrow keys inside the position boxes; the g key: go straight to a ticker, with auto-complete.
  f toggles full screen (the monitor the window is on; its place and size come back on f, Esc, or anything that breaks out of it); fullscale in wincan.ini; + and - size the text there.
  A dashed line follows the mouse up and down the chart (small window and full screen) and the price under it is shown at the top centre.
  The window takes the keyboard when the mouse is over it (no click needed), and no longer draws a blue border while it has it.
  Volume candles no longer overlap (they do not in KuCoin): each keeps its own width, side by side, so fewer candles are on show when some are wide.
  The top centre shows the clock; with the mouse over the chart it shows the price under the mouse and the date and time of the candle under it (no vertical line).
  The keyboard: a window that believed it had the keyboard when it did not now notices at the next movement of the mouse and takes it again; a plain request for the foreground is tried before borrowing the input of another thread.
  Within the range of the candles the pointer snaps to the middle of the nearest candle (standard and volume candles) and is held there until the user's real motion would have left the candle, then snaps to the next; the candle is brighter with a halo.
  Vertical snap, the same way: at a candle the pointer is held on its open, high, low, close or an indicator line within a few pixels, until the real motion would have left those few pixels; the exact value is shown.
  The top centre shows the date and time of the candle under the mouse while the mouse is over the candles, and the current date and time otherwise (one or the other, never both; the price under the mouse beside it while over the chart).
  Hovering a heat strip bar shows it brighter on a haze, and at the top centre its side, its per cent from the present price (i to i+1) and the price range that is.
